Link SIP Python extension modules against Python::Module instead of
Python::Python.

Python::Python links the shared libpython into the extension modules.
On macOS, conda-forge's python executable is statically linked, so the
extension's libpython.dylib is loaded as a second, uninitialized copy of
the Python runtime. Its thread state (_Py_tss_tstate) is NULL, so the
very first call into it (PyModule_Create2 in PyInit__core) dereferences
tstate->interp and segfaults (EXC_BAD_ACCESS at address 0x10) on
`import qgis.core`.

Python::Module is the CMake target intended for extension modules: it
does not link libpython and on Apple platforms passes
`-undefined dynamic_lookup`, so Python symbols resolve from the host
interpreter at load time (same as the PyQt5 extension modules).
On Linux it adds no library and on Windows it still links the import
library, so behavior there is unchanged.

See https://github.com/conda-forge/qgis-feedstock/pull/505

diff --git a/cmake/SIPMacros.cmake b/cmake/SIPMacros.cmake
--- a/cmake/SIPMacros.cmake
+++ b/cmake/SIPMacros.cmake
@@ -193,7 +193,7 @@ MACRO(BUILD_SIP_PYTHON_MODULE MODULE_NAME SIP_FILES EXTRA_OBJECTS)
   ENDIF (${SIP_VERSION_STR} VERSION_LESS 5.0.0)
 
   SET_TARGET_PROPERTIES(${_logical_name} PROPERTIES CXX_VISIBILITY_PRESET default)
-  TARGET_LINK_LIBRARIES(${_logical_name} Python::Python)
+  TARGET_LINK_LIBRARIES(${_logical_name} Python::Module)
   TARGET_LINK_LIBRARIES(${_logical_name} ${EXTRA_LINK_LIBRARIES})
   SET_TARGET_PROPERTIES(${_logical_name} PROPERTIES PREFIX "" OUTPUT_NAME ${_child_module_name})
 
